# Line-ending normalisation. Binding for every clone, including Windows hosts.
#
# Why this file exists (M0-T0.1, conflict C11): this repo is developed on a Windows host with
# core.autocrlf=true, so without an explicit policy a fresh clone materialises the Makefile and
# scripts/*.sh with CRLF line endings. GNU make rejects a CRLF recipe and /bin/sh under WSL2
# rejects a CRLF script, so the checkout is broken before the first target runs. CI cannot catch
# this: GitHub's actions/checkout sets core.autocrlf=false, so the Linux runner always sees LF
# regardless of what a developer's clone contains. Normalising in the repository rather than in
# each developer's git config is the only place the guarantee holds for everyone.
#
# Already-tracked files keep their recorded eol until the working tree is re-normalised; run that
# as a deliberate, reviewable change rather than as a side effect of adding this file.

* text=auto eol=lf
Makefile text eol=lf
*.sh text eol=lf
*.env text eol=lf
*.mk text eol=lf
*.py text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.mjs text eol=lf
*.ts text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.rego text eol=lf
*.pdf binary
